ng like yeah  we will agree to play by these rules so  that we can drive cars into each other's  countries and stuff and one of the  stipulations of the vienna convention is  that your license plate could only use  the latin alphabet like the vanilla  ascii english alphabet now license  plates in the soviet union in 1965 look  like this  and i'm guessing that there was a  meeting somewhere where because the  soviet union signed the vienna  convention they're like yep we're fine  and i'm guessing at some point someone  said but uh what if you need to take  your car out of the soviet union and  they went to a comrade  you cannot drive car out of soviet union  but  if you did when you got to the border if  you had all your travel papers to be  able to leave they'd give you temporary  license plates and presumably if the  license plates didn't come back your  family went to the gulag so you would  bring your car back there um but then  when the soviet union you know kind of  collapsed in the 1990s all of the former  soviet republics ukraine russia belarus  they all overhauled their vehicle  registration system and so now their  license plates look like this  and what they did  is they took the russian or the cyrillic  and the english alphabet and they looked  at the set of not the set of letters but  the set of glyphs the shapes that exist  in both of those alphabets and that is  the subset that are used on vehicle  registration plates in uh this exact  subset is in ukraine russia is almost  the same belarus is almost the same but  this is the set of letters you'll see on  ukrainian license plates and if you  rearrange those  you get the word pike matchbox  so next time somebody says to you hey  i'm going to send you a file in plain  text you just say to them do you know  pike matchbox  and if they say yep then you're good  they know about big endian little ending  utf-8 udf 16 the history of ascii  cohuept character encoding cyrillic code  pages and if they say no what